    "title": "Detroit woman pleads guilty to organizing Ulta theft ring - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Shajaka Shelton ,\n8 hours ago\nLANSING, Mich. (WLNS) — A Detroit woman is facing up to 20 years in prison after pleading guilty to organizing an Ulta Beauty theft ring that targeted stores across Metro Detroit, Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel announced Wednesday.\nCheaira Broughton, 38, entered her guilty plea to one count of conducting a criminal enterprise earlier this month after being charged in November 2025.\nNessel says Broughton organized a small group that conducted more than thirteen “push out” thefts — where an individual fills a cart or basket with items and walks out of the store to a getaway car without paying — at Ulta locations between October and November 2023.\nBroughton would then sell the stolen items on Facebook Marketplace. Total losses to these stores exceeded $30,000, Nessel says.\nBroughton is expected to be sentenced by Judge Wanda Evans in the 3rd Circuit Court in Wayne County on April 17.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.",
    "summary": "LANSING, Mich. (WLNS) — A Detroit woman is facing up to 20 years in prison after pleading guilty to organizing an Ulta Beauty theft ring that targeted stores across Metro Detroit, Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel announced Wednesday. Cheaira Broughton, 38, entered her guilty plea to one count of conducting a criminal enterprise earlier this month after being charged in November 2025.",
